east relative transparency about  them  the ones that are the big question marks  are the ones that  are from the period 2006 to 2014  and that was a time when donald trump  was spending huge amounts of cash  that he doesn't tend to have as we're  learning  and he spent huge amounts of cash buying  or developing or improving properties  and for a long time there's been a  general question about where the hell  the cash came from  and now with the bombshell new york  times story we only have more questions  about where did the cash come from given  how precarious donald trump's financial  position appears to be  based on the documents that the new york  times obtained when we find out  and as we did previously when we know  that donald trump paid 200 million  dollars in cash  to renovate that turnberry golf course  in scotland  where did 200 million in cash come from  because it doesn't appear that donald  trump had it  and now there is even more skepticism  about it uh trump is claiming hey we  just had 200 million in cash sitting  around but it is extraordinarily  difficult to believe that's the case now  and indeed people who know donald  trump's businesses and his finances have  said  there's no way trump had 200 million in  cash particularly at that time  you can check out adam davidson's work  for the new yorker  as one example of someone who really  understands the finances of trump  and the trump organization and to be  clear we don't  know that these are particularly sketchy  debts  tied to trump's claim of having all this  cash  we just know that at a sort of general  level  it's not believable that trump had this  cash at the time  we don't know where the cash came from  and we know that trump owes a ton of  money so so it's  it's all of those three things put  together now beyond that  not all of trump's deutsche bank debts  are created equal  there are these deutsche bank debts that  like i said are tied to a specific  mortgage to a specific property  but at some point during that same  period  2006 to 2014 donald trump went in the  direction  of private banking at deutsche bank and  at that point the paper trail starts to  get  shadier and shadowier and we just lack  a lot of information about it so there's  sort of a double bottom line here or two  different bottom lines one bottom line  is now with the revelations of the new  york times bombshell tax story  are there even more questions about  who trump owes money to where he got  outrageous amounts of cash that don't  seem to fit his financial situation  during that period 06 to 14. yeah  absolutely  even more questions about it which raise  even more national security questions in  terms of a trump who could be  could be manipulable or subject to  coercion because of the amount of money  that he owes yes  the second bottom line is is this going  to matter in terms of the 2020 election  i hate to say it particularly with more  time now having passed  since the sunday story i think the  answer is no  i i do not think at this point in time  that donald trump is going to be hurt in  any significant way by the new york  times bombshell tax story and what's  crazy about that is  for years we've been saying  the suspicion is trump pays  no or very little taxes trump's not as  wealthy as he thinks as he claims to be  trump owes a lot of people money it was  all proven true by the new york times  bombshell story  and yet the people who care are mostly  already not voting for donald trump  and the people who are voting for donald  trump aren't going to be influenced by  it either way so as much as it pains me  to say it because of what it says about  the american voting public at this point  and it says something very very  bad and sad i don't think that the tax  story is going to make a difference if  you disagree with me  i want to hear from you and again as far  as debates are concerned  vp debate on wednesday kamala harris  versus mike pence  i predict there will be